### Session 5 (2026-06-25)
**Lesson:** [0005 — Block Connections: Backend](lessons/0005-block-connections-backend.html)
**Covered:**
- Three-layer model: data (connected_to pointers) · visual (WireItem) · signal (pyqtSignal emissions)
- ConnectionPort: parent_block back-reference, port_id, connected_to, fractional x/y positioning via add_port()
- The symmetry invariant: A.connected_to=B ↔ B.connected_to=A — always both sides or neither
- connect_to() / disconnect() contract — why peer must be nulled before self
- WireItem: ZValue=-1 (drawn below blocks), update_position() uses scenePos() not pos()
- Why scenePos() not pos(): child items live in parent-local coordinates; scene coords needed for cross-block lines
- Three-event mouse state machine: press→_start_wire / move→update temp / release→connect_ports or _cancel_wire
- _port_at(): iterates items(pos) front-to-back, skips _temp_wire (ZValue 100), returns first ConnectionPort
- event.accept() in mousePressEvent/moveEvent is load-bearing — without it, CanvasView's RubberBandDrag hijacks the gesture
- Temp wire: ZValue=100, NoButton accepted, not selectable — so it doesn't eat subsequent hit tests
- connect_ports() guards: self-loop and already-connected; creates WireItem then calls connect_to()
- _remove_wire() order: disconnect both ports (data) → pop from _wires (dict) → removeItem (visual) → signal
- to_dict()/from_dict() via port_map keyed by (block_id, port_id) tuples
- Why wires silently drop on reload: device_type mismatch → add_block() returns None → port_map miss → wire skipped
- clear() override: super().clear() deletes C++ items; dicts must be reset or RuntimeError on next access

