### Session 6 (2026-06-25)
**Lesson:** [0006 — Hardware Docs &amp; Custom Blocks](lessons/0006-hardware-docs-and-custom-blocks.html)
**Covered:**
- Two-folder system: hardware_docs/ (reference docs, offline) vs custom_blocks/ (executable JSON specs)
- hardware_docs/ layout convention: vendor/model/programmer_reference.pdf + scpi_commands.md + notes.md
- JSON spec format: name, manufacturer, model, color, ports (x/y fractions), commands (key → response)
- Port coordinate fractions: x=0.0 left edge, x=1.0 right edge, y=0.5 vertical center
- Three command response types: literal (static string), empty (write-only), template ({0}, {1}, etc.)
- BlockSpec.from_dict() normalizes all command keys to UPPERCASE (str(k).upper())
- _make_handler(): checks for '{' to determine literal vs template; falls back to template string on IndexError
- _CustomDevice inherits from BaseDevice — gets common IEEE 488.2 handlers (*IDN? etc.) automatically
- Discovery pipeline: discover_specs() → make_custom_block_class() → register_custom_blocks() → BLOCK_REGISTRY
- PACKAGED_DIR = silmulator/custom_blocks/, USER_DIR = ~/.silmulator/custom_blocks/
- First-seen wins on duplicate names; bad JSON files are silently skipped (non-fatal)
- JSON spec vs Python subclass: JSON for static protocol verification, Python for stateful simulation
- The upgrade path: start with JSON, promote to Python subclass when state is needed

